description:
When running R code from Jupyter notebooks using [IRkernel](https://github.com/IRkernel/IRkernel), `print()`ed output only appears after one cell has finished.

Take the following example, executed in a Jupyter notebook:

```r
f <- function() {
    for (i in 1:3) {
        print(i)
        # flush.console()
        Sys.sleep(1)
    }
}
```

Executing `f()` in another cell will only show continuous output if `flush.console()` becomes uncommented. Real-life use cases include running `install.packages(...)` and not getting any update as well as using streaming functions that are supposed to provide continuous output (see IRkernel/IRkernel#625).

Therefore it would be great if {evaluate} could autoflush after encountering a newline (`\n`, `\r`).

```R
test_that("on.exit is evaluated at end of code", {
  ev <- evaluate::evaluate(c(
    "on.exit(print('bye'))",
    "print('hi')"
  ))
  expect_output_types(ev, c("source", "source", "text", "text"))
})

test_that("return causes an early return", {
  ev <- evaluate::evaluate(c(
    "1 + 1",
    "return()",
    "2 + 2"
  ))
  expect_output_types(ev, c("source", "text", "source"))
})
```

Hello,
I am writing web application which needs to communicate with R server to do some computation. 
I use Plumber to communicate, where I am using your function `evaluate ` to execute script sent as parameter and send back outputs, messages and graphics. The whole code in Plumber is as follows
```
#' @post /executescript
#' @serializer lmJSON 
function(txtScript,data){
library(evaluate)
evaluate(txtScript)
}
```
As a result I get serialized to JSON "list" from R with all outputs, messages and graphics. The graphics is represented with raw data, ie. 
```
 {
                    "type": "raw",
                    "attributes": {
                        "pkgName": {
                            "type": "character",
                            "attributes": {},
                            "value": [
                                "graphics"
                            ]
                        }
                    },
                    "value": "AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAADgPwEAAAD/////bwAAA .... and many many more, mostly A values, with '\n' characters (KBytes of data)
}
```
and I don't know how to deal with it. It looks like I got already serialized binaries (to me of unknown format) and I do not know how to handle it. I would like to be able to use it in `img` element with `src=data:image/png;base64,ivBOR...etc....`. 

Is there a way to find out where  is a binary for plot in evaluate results, so that I could write my own serializer to that case, if there is no one for PNG ? Is this what handle_output was designed for?

Is there any way to store the return value of a function call, even when it is invisible? E.g:

```
library(evaluate)

test <- function(){
  invisible("FOO")
}

myenv <- new.env()
myhandler <- new_output_handler(value=function(value){
  assign("value", value, envir=myenv);
  evaluate:::render(value);
});

out <- evaluate("test()", output_handler=myhandler);
ls(myenv)
# character(0)
```

In this snippet above, the value is not stored in `myenv` because it was invisible. Ignoring invisible values makes sense for knitr, but for some other cases we might actually be interested in this value. 

Perhaps it would make sense to do the filtering of invisible values inside `evaluate:::render` instead of inside `evaluate_call`? That way the default behavior doesn't change, but the user can override this using the `output_handler`.

